import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../../../components/TableOfContents.astro';
import StatusBadge from '../../../components/StatusBadge.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { url } from '../../../utils/url';
import prismShot from '../../../assets/captures/launcher-prism.png';
---
<BaseLayout
  lang="en"
  translated
  title="PRISM 1.0.0: perceptual rendering"
  description="PRISM 1.0.0 is packaged for Windows and Linux with the PRISM-LDK PrismLdk Unreal plugin."
>
  <PageHeader
    lang="en"
    index="08"
    eyebrow="Architecture · PRISM"
    title="Rendering."
    lead="PRISM (Perceptual Rendering & Interactive Simulation Module) gives form and presence to the simulated world. PRISM 1.0.0 is delivered for Windows and Linux."
    crumbs={[{ label: 'Architecture', href: 'technique/' }, { label: 'PRISM' }]}
  />

  <div class="w" style="padding-top:8px">
    <TableOfContents lang="en" />
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <p style="margin-top:0"><StatusBadge status="disponible" lang="en" /> <span class="mu">PRISM 1.0.0 · PRISM-LDK 1.0.0</span></p>
      <div class="cc"><p><strong>PRISM-LDK 1.0.0</strong> (Unreal module <code>PrismLdk</code>) exposes SYNE contracts to Blueprint. <strong>PRISM 1.0.0</strong>, built around the plugin, is packaged for Windows and Linux. Packaging confirms delivery of the applications; it does not mean every rendering function in the project vision is complete or validated.</p></div>

      <div class="sh"><b>8.1</b> Role</div>
      <h2>Making dynamics visible.</h2>
      <p>
        Where SYNE computes and ECHOS measures, PRISM <strong>renders the simulated world as a 3D scene</strong>.
        The Unreal project owns the environment, objects and visual representations, along with the interface and
        interactions used to inspect the world. It consists of the PRISM project and the integration plugin
        <strong>PRISM-LDK</strong>.
      </p>

      <figure class="shot">
        <Image src={prismShot} alt="PRISM rendering preview"  loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>PRISM rendering shown from the Launcher. This capture presents the interface in the repository; the delivered scope and evolving features are described below.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>8.2</b> What PRISM does</div>
      <h2>A world computed by SYNE, rendered by Unreal.</h2>
      <p>PRISM receives the description of a prepared world, then the full snapshots and events sent by SYNE. It uses them to build or update the scene: each agent remains associated with its SYNE identifier, and displayed positions and states follow the authoritative state of the received tick. Visual effects can signal events without applying mutations a second time when they are already reflected in a snapshot.</p>
      <p>PRISM can participate in an experiment's lifecycle: connect, prepare the presentation, confirm it is ready, then request start, pause, resume or stop using the contract. Decision-making, world progression and authoritative data remain in SYNE.</p>
      <div class="ov"><table class="tb"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Element</th><th scope="col">Responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Environment and objects</td><td>The PRISM project chooses how to represent the logical world's dimensions and data in 3D.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Agents</td><td>The scene creates and updates their representation while preserving their SYNE identifier.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Interaction</td><td>The interface can control the lifecycle and present received states and events.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Simulation</td><td>SYNE alone owns the rules, decisions, ticks and authoritative state.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p class="mu">The Unreal project chooses its scene, scale, navigation and interface; the plugin does not generate them automatically.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>8.3</b> PRISM-LDK / PrismLdk</div>
      <h2>The Unreal integration kit.</h2>
      <p><strong>PRISM-LDK</strong> means <em>LIVEX Development Kit</em>; <code>PrismLdk</code> is the Unreal module name. This C++ plugin connects SYNE to the Unreal project. It exposes contracts, structures, control functions and events to Blueprints so PRISM can connect the transport and respond to updates without reimplementing the protocol.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Local WebSocket on port 5180: world descriptions, snapshots, deltas and events;</li>
        <li>Local HTTP on port 5181: control commands and status;</li>
        <li>Blueprint nodes to obtain the subsystem, configure and connect the client, send commands and receive results;</li>
        <li>an actor registry indexed by SYNE identifier, exposed through the plugin's registry subsystem.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The plugin is packaged separately for Unreal Engine 5.8 projects. By itself it creates no terrain, actors, collisions, navigation, user interface or animations. It does not contain a parallel simulation engine.</p>
      <p><a class="bt" href="https://github.com/donovan-dev-web/LIVEX/blob/main/docs/prism/PRISM_UNREAL_IMPLEMENTATION.md" rel="noopener">Blueprint integration guide ↗</a> <a class="bt" href="https://github.com/donovan-dev-web/LIVEX/blob/main/docs/prism/TRANSPORT_API.md" rel="noopener">Transport contracts ↗</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>8.4</b> Delivery and scope</div>
      <h2>Release status.</h2>
      <p>PRISM 1.0.0 is packaged for Windows and Linux and runs from the distribution without Unreal Engine installed. The 76 PRISM/PrismLdk Automation Specs executed for the release are validated: 71 passed, 5 passed with warnings, 0 failed.</p>
      <p>Packaging and tests validate the behavior covered by their scenarios; they do not mean every feature described in the rendering specifications is complete. The roadmap details the work still evolving.</p>
      <p class="mu" style="font-size:0.82rem">
        The project's roadmap is authoritative for the steps ahead.
      </p>
      <div class="cc">
        <p>Do not assume that local protections in the engine or mock secure distribution of the PRISM project. See <a href="https://github.com/donovan-dev-web/LIVEX/blob/main/SECURITY.md" rel="noopener">SECURITY.md</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:18px"><a class="bt" href={url('technique/', 'en')}>← Overview</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
